{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's claim defines a binary iff: resumption is permitted when trailing bytes are identifiable as an incomplete uncommitted fragment (non-event), and refused when trailing bytes are indistinguishable from a corrupted complete event. But SRC_001's own countercondition identifies a third case — budget exhaustion between a data write and a mandatory commit record, leaving a structurally complete yet logically uncommitted event. Such bytes are neither an 'incomplete, uncommitted fragment' (they are structurally complete) nor 'indistinguishable from a corrupted complete event' (the checksum may be valid). The claim's iff leaves this intermediate case unadjudicated, failing the problem's demand to determine 'exactly when' resumption may proceed.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Budget exhaustion after a full event body and checksum are written but before the commit record. The framing protocol sees a valid checksum and complete structure, so trailing bytes are neither a partial fragment nor a corrupted event. SRC_001's iff does not resolve whether resumption is safe.","outcome":"unaddressed_gap"}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's transfer claim asserts that causal fingerprint matching from distributed systems 'applies to logical causal continuity verification in reasoning runs,' and the disanalogies confirm reasoning resumption 'simply matches fingerprints and skips non-events.' But if budget exhaustion occurs before any complete event is framed, there are zero causal fingerprints to match against. The analogy provides no mechanism for this case — fingerprint matching is vacuous when no prior checkpoint or complete event exists. The problem requires adjudicating resumption in this edge case, and the analogy's central transfer claim breaks down exactly here.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"A reasoning run exhausts its token budget after emitting 80%% of its first event. No complete event exists; there are no causal fingerprints to match. The quarantine mechanism can skip the partial bytes, but the fingerprint-matching transfer claim provides no verification path.","outcome":"analogy_fails_at_zero_event_boundary"}],"target_alias":"SRC_002"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003's claim permits resumption when framing 'can deterministically quarantine the truncated bytes of a partial event' and refuses only when framing 'lacks the delimiters or checksums to deterministically distinguish a complete event from a partial one.' This framework addresses only the partial-vs-complete structural distinction. A structurally complete but logically uncommitted event (e.g., valid checksum, full body, but no commit record) is distinguishable from a partial, so SRC_003 would classify it as a complete event and permit resumption — yet treating an uncommitted event as committed corrupts the append-only identity of recorded events. SRC_003 never considers the distinction between structural completeness and logical commitment, leaving a resumption-permitting gap the problem's 'exactly when' standard requires closed.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"An event with length-prefix, full body, and valid checksum is written, but the mandatory commit/tombstone record after it is never written due to budget exhaustion. Framing can distinguish this from a partial event (it is structurally complete), so SRC_003 permits resumption. But the event is logically uncommitted, so resumption corrupts the append-only identity.","outcome":"false_permit_for_logically_uncommitted_event"}],"target_alias":"SRC_003"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_004's third countercondition mandates refusal 'if no complete event was framed before exhaustion,' because resumption 'cannot anchor causal continuity.' But the problem's standard is about resuming 'without corrupting the append-only identity of the recorded events.' When zero complete events exist and the framing protocol deterministically classifies the trailing partial bytes as non-events, there are no recorded events whose identity could be corrupted — append-only integrity is vacuously preserved. SRC_004's refusal criterion conflates causal anchoring (a desirable but separate property) with the integrity-preservation standard the problem actually specifies, making it an over-restrictive rule not justified by the problem as stated.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Budget exhaustion leaves 50 bytes of a partial event with no prior complete events. The framing protocol uses self-delimiting checksums and deterministically classifies the 50 bytes as a non-event. No recorded events exist to corrupt. SRC_004 refuses resumption, but the append-only identity of recorded events is vacuously intact.","outcome":"unjustified_refusal_when_identity_is_vacuously_preserved"}],"target_alias":"SRC_004"}]}